.TH "" "" "" ""
.SH package.yml(5) \(en Solus package build format
.SS SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
The Solus package build format, with YAML syntax.
.EE
.SS DESCRIPTION
The primary format of package builds in Solus, \f[CR]package.yml(5)\f[R]
provides a modern, declarative, and structured format in which to define
metadata and steps, to convert a build operation into a package.
.PP
\f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] will use the advanced patterns system and some
very specific internal rules to automatically split the package into
multiple packages, refered to here as \(lqsubpackages\(rq.
.SS CONFIGURATION FORMAT
\f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] uses the YAML file format.
However, this is a very specialised flavour of YAML with very specific
extensions and types.
.PP
\f[CR]Types\f[R]
.PP
Some of the specialised types expected by \f[CR]package.yml(5)\f[R] are
explained below.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]dict(s)\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
This is a set of one or more \f[CR]key\f[R]:\f[CR]value\f[R] mappings.
These are always in a list format.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]multimap\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
This is a \f[CR]key\f[R]:\f[CR]value\f[R] mapping where the
\f[CR]key\f[R] is IMPLICIT.
That is to say, it is acceptable to omit the key.
A single value passed as the \f[CR]value\f[R] will set the implicit
key\(cqs component.
.PP
In all instances the implicit key is the \f[CR]main package\f[R].
The \f[CR]multimap\f[R] format can accept a list instead of a string as
a value, and each item in that list also follows the implicit key
policy.
.PP
For each item in the list, if the \f[CR]key\f[R] is EXPLICIT, by using
\f[CR]dict\f[R] style \f[CR]key\f[R]:\f[CR]value\f[R] mapping, then the
key specified will be used.
.PP
Within \f[CR]ypkg(1)\f[R], that key is always a package name.
This name should be the shortform name, not the fully qualified name,
i.e the subpackage name without the \f[CR]name\f[R] prefix.
.PP
As a special exception to the subpackage rule, keys beginning with
\f[CR]\(ha\f[R] will result in an explicitly named package lookup,
i.e.\ one that doesn\(cqt follow the subpackage convention.
This should be used only in rare cases where it is illogical to share a
common root name, or a migration with name changes from a legacy format
is too complex.
.PP
This allows a general key to accept sane defaults, but also allows the
key to be extended to override attributes of a subpackage.
.RE
.PP
\f[CR]Mandatory Keys\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]name\f[R] [string]
.RS 2
.PP
Set the name of the package.
In general this should try to match the upstream source name wherever
possible.
All subpackages generated by \f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] will have this
\f[CR]name\f[R] as a prefix.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]version\f[R] [string]
.RS 2
.PP
The version of the software being packaged.
This should match the upstream version, i.e.\ that of the tarball or git
tag.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]release\f[R] [integer]
.RS 2
.PP
Packages within Solus are updated by their \f[B]release number\f[R].
This number must start at 1 in new packages, and be incremented for
every new update or change to the package that is published.
.PP
It is perfectly acceptable to push an update with a \f[B]lower
version\f[R] by bumping the release number.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]license\f[R] [string(s)]
.RS 2
.PP
One or more strings that identify the software license of this package.
This should in most cases be a recognised \f[CR]SPDX\f[R] license name.
.IP
.EX
  * https://spdx.org/licenses/
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]source\f[R] [dict(s)]
.RS 2
.PP
This key expects a list of \f[CR]key\f[R]:\f[CR]value\f[R] mappings, to
declare the sources that this package will use.
A \(lqsimple source\(rq could be a tarball, where the key is the
upstream URL, and the value must be a valid \f[CR]sha256sum(1)\f[R] for
that tarball.
.PP
You can list multiple sources and they will all be fetched for the build
process, but only the first will be extracted.
You can locate these other sources during your build with the
\f[CR]$sources\f[R] variable.
.PP
\f[CR]git(1)\f[R] sources are also supported, and can be identified by
prefixing the URI with \f[CR]git|\f[R].
The expected value should be a commit, sha reference, or a tag.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]component\f[R] [multimap]
.RS 2
.PP
This key sets the package component, that is to say, the logical unit of
organisation that it belongs to.
Check \f[CR]eopkg lc\f[R] for existing component names.
.PP
\f[CR]component\f[R] is a multimap key, therefore if passed a single
string value it will set the component for the main package.
However, you may instead pass a list of the subpackage names, and set
their component individually using the map \f[B]value\f[R].
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]summary\f[R] [multimap]
.RS 2
.PP
Set the \(lqshort\(rq description of a package, i.e.\ a one line
explanation of what an item is.
Use the subpackage names in the explicit key for this multimap to
override subpackage summaries.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]description\f[R] [multimap]
.RS 2
.PP
Set the full description of a package, i.e.\ a more in depth explanation
of the software/package\(cqs functionality.
This is the large description that will be displayed in \f[CR]eopkg\f[R]
and the \f[CR]solus\-sc\f[R].
Use the subpackage names in the explicit key for this multimap to
override subpackage descriptions.
.RE
.PP
\f[CR]Optional Keys\f[R]
.PP
These keys are not mandatory to a \f[CR]package.yml\f[R] file, but may
be used to configure additional functionality.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]builddeps\f[R] [list]
.RS 2
.PP
Specifies the build dependencies required to actually make this package
build in an isolated environment (\f[CR]solbuild(1)\f[R]).
.PP
You may use full package names here, though it is preferable to use the
\f[CR]pkg\-config(1)\f[R] names.
.PP
\f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] understands pkgconfig dependencies denoted
inside either the \f[CR]pkgconfig($name)\f[R] identifier, or
\f[CR]pkgconfig32($name)\f[R] for emul32 build dependencies.
.PP
It is not required to list any package here that exists in the
\f[CR]system.base\f[R] or \f[CR]system.devel\f[R] component.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]checkdeps\f[R] [list]
.RS 2
.PP
Specifies the build and/or run dependencies required to build and/or run
the tests of this package in an isolated environment
(\f[CR]solbuild(1)\f[R]).
.PP
You may use full package names here, though it is preferable to use the
\f[CR]pkg\-config(1)\f[R] names.
.PP
\f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] understands pkgconfig dependencies denoted
inside either the \f[CR]pkgconfig($name)\f[R] identifier, or
\f[CR]pkgconfig32($name)\f[R] for emul32 build dependencies.
.PP
It is not required to list any package here that exists in the
\f[CR]system.base\f[R] or \f[CR]system.devel\f[R] component.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]clang\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
Set this key to \f[CR]yes\f[R] to force building this package with the
\f[CR]clang\f[R] compiler.
The build environment will be configured to use \f[CR]clang\f[R] as the
\f[CR]$CC\f[R] and \f[CR]clang++\f[R] as the \f[CR]$CXX\f[R] variables.
.PP
By default this key is set to \f[CR]no\f[R].
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]ccache\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
If configured correctly, \f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] will automatically
use \f[CR]ccache(1)\f[R].
When using \f[CR]solbuild(1)\f[R] this is almost always the case.
However, there may be some cases when ccache can break the build, or is
ill advised.
.PP
Whilst the default value of this key is \f[CR]yes\f[R], you can force
disable the use of ccache by setting it to \f[CR]no\f[R].
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]devel\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
Force all built packages to belong to the \f[CR]system.devel\f[R]
component.
This will become deprecated in future, and currently defaults to
\f[CR]no\f[R].
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]extract\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
By default, \f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] will extract all sources listed in
the file.
If this is undesirable, set this key to \f[CR]no\f[R] to disable this
automatic extraction.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]autodep\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
After a build has finished, \f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] will automatically
scan the package files to determine dependencies between the package and
any of it\(cqs subpackages, and to external packages in the build
environment.
.PP
This is essential in most cases, as it allows packages to benefit from
automatic dependencies and ensures the user always gets all of the
packages needed to run this software when installing it.
.PP
If for any reason you need to disable this functionality, i.e.\ for
bootstrapping or sideloading, set this key to \f[CR]no\f[R].
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]emul32\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
\f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] can optionally build your package in a
multilib configuration.
If this key is set to \f[CR]yes\f[R], the buildset will double, and the
first build configuration will be set up for a \f[CR]32\-bit\f[R]
(\(lqemul32\(rq) build.
ypkg will automatically split off \f[CR]\-32bit\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-32bit\-devel\f[R] subpackages in this instance, using the
\f[CR]/usr/lib32\f[R] library directory.
It will also add additional build dependencies automatically for 32\-bit
builds.
.PP
By default, this key is set to \f[CR]no\f[R].
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]libsplit\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
The default patterns include logic to split subpackages according to the
library files in library directory.
It is standard practice for ypkg to split \f[CR]*.so\f[R] symlinks into
the automatic \f[CR]devel\f[R] subpackage, along with other development
assets such as \f[CR]pkgconfig\f[R] and \f[CR]*.h\f[R] files.
.PP
Some software packages provide \f[CR]*.so\f[R] files in the libdir that
are not symlinks, or are required for \(lqmain\(rq operation.
In this instance you can set this key to \f[CR]no\f[R] to disable this
pattern.
.PP
By default, this key is set to \f[CR]yes\f[R], and should only be
disabled if truly required.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]rundeps\f[R] [multimap]
.RS 2
.PP
Provide a list of additional runtime dependencies for the main package.
These names should be fully qualified package names in the list, even
for subpackages.
.PP
If the EXPLICIT multimap key is set, then the runtime dependencies will
be added to the subpackage instead.
Note that you can pass a list or a single string value to the EXPLICIT
rundep.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]replaces\f[R] [multimap]
.RS 2
.PP
When exported in the package index, this will indicate to the package
manager that THIS package now replaces the name in the value.
.PP
You may also set \f[CR]replaces\f[R] on subpackages using the multimap
notation.
Only one value per subpackage is allowed.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]patterns\f[R] [multimap]
.RS 2
.PP
Control package splitting and dynamically generate subpackages.
The EXPLICIT key is used to specify the new (or existing!)
subpackage name.
The \(lqpattern\(rq is a shell compatible \f[CR]glob(3)\f[R] expression.
.PP
All files captured by this expression will then end up in that
subpackage.
Each successive pattern takes priority over the one listed before it, so
if your first pattern unavoidably captures files you need in ANOTHER
subpackage, simply list that pattern later.
.PP
\f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] ensures that a file cannot belong to multiple
packages, and that the last specified pattern, if matching, ALWAYS wins.
It is even possible to suppress generation of the main package, by
pattern globbing \f[CR]/*\f[R] to a subpackage.
This will not cause any breakage.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]permanent\f[R] [list]
.RS 2
.PP
A list of patterns used to mark files as permanent.
Any file within the resulting binary packages that matches the path
pattern, is marked as a permanent file.
.PP
These files will not be removed from the filesystem when upgrading or
reinstalling the package.
They will persist during standard upgrade operations.
.PP
This should only be used in critical chain packages such as the kernel
or drivers, where the domain of control is outside of the package
manager, and the package is simply used as an update delivery mechanism.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]strip\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
By default, this key is enabled, and as a result
\f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] will automatically strip files after the build
has done, including \f[CR]ELF\f[R] libraries & binaries.
.PP
In most cases, stripping should remain enabled.
However, there are known cases when stripping should be avoided, such as
when complying with a distribution policy of binary only software, or
when dealing with files that only appear to be standard ELF files.
.PP
The Go programming language generates \f[CR]*.a\f[R] archive files that
under no circumstance should be stripped, and there are likely other
cases.
This key, when set to \f[CR]no\f[R], will disable any and all stripping.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]lastrip\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
By default, this key is enabled, and will result in \f[CR]*.la\f[R]
libtool files being stripped from the build.
However in some very rare cases these files need to be preserved because
they\(cqre not \f[B]true\f[R] libtool scripts which led to the creation
of successful \f[CR].so\f[R] linkage.
.PP
If in doubt, omit this option where possible.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]mancompress\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
By default, this key is enabled.
Enables compression of man/info pages using zstd at the maximum
compression level, to decrease the installed size of the package on
disk.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]debug\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
By default, this key is enabled, and as a result
\f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] will automatically create resulting
\f[CR]\-dbginfo\f[R] packages where it can.
.PP
In the majority of cases, this is the desired behaviour in full build
environments, such as a build server.
However in very rare cases, this may cause problems for the package,
especially if it contains binaries that have not been bootstrapped with
the native toolchain.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]avx2\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
If set, the package will be rebuilt again with the
\f[CR]x86\-64\-v3\f[R] microarchitecture to enable libraries to be
optimised to use newer hardware instructions such as \f[B]Advanced
Vector Extensions\f[R].
From baseline (\f[CR]x86\-64\f[R]) to v3 (\f[CR]x86\-64\-v3\f[R]) it
allows the compiler to use additional instructions such as, but not
limited to; SSE4.2, SSSE3, POPCNT, CMPXCHG16B, MOVBE and AVX2.
.PP
The build will be configured to make use of the Glibc HWCaps (hardware
capabilities) feature, by placing the libraries into the library
directory suffix of \f[CR]glibc\-hwcaps/x86\-64\-v3\f[R]
i.e.\ \f[CR]/usr/lib64/glibc\-hwcaps/x86\-64\-v3\f[R].
.PP
These libraries will be automatically loaded on the Solus installation
if the hardware supports the \f[CR]x86\-64\-v3\f[R] microarchitecture.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]optimize\f[R] [list]
.RS 2
.PP
Valid keys are restricted to:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]speed\f[R]: Optimise this package for speed performance
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]size\f[R]: Optimize the package build solely for size.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]no\-bind\-now\f[R]: Configure the package to disable certain
flags, where RELRO is unsupported.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]no\-frame\-pointer\f[R]: Disable
\f[CR]\-fno\-omit\-frame\-pointer\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-mno\-omit\-leaf\-frame\-pointer\f[R] compiler flags
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]no\-symbolic\f[R]: Disable \f[CR]\-Wl,\-Bsymbolic\-functions\f[R]
linker flag
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]unroll\-loops\f[R]: Enable \f[CR]\-funroll\-loops\f[R].
Use this sparingly, only when it provides benefit.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]runpath\f[R]: Enable \f[CR]\-Wl,\-\-enable\-new\-dtags\f[R] to
make linker use RUNPATH\(cqs instead of RPATH\(cqs.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]avx256\f[R]: Disables \f[CR]\-mprefer\-vector\-width=128\f[R] in
avx2 builds
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]thin\-lto\f[R]: Enable Thin Link Time Optimization
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]lto\f[R]: Enable Link Time Optimization
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]icf\-safe\f[R]: Enable \f[CR]\-Wl,\-\-icf=safe\f[R] to utilize the
safe Identical Code Folding linker optimization.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]icf\-all\f[R]: Enable \f[CR]\-Wl,\-\-icf=all\f[R] to utilize the
Identical Code Folding linker optimization.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]function\-sections\f[R]: Enables \f[CR]\-ffunction\-sections\f[R]
to generate a seperate ELF section for each function.
Recommended for icf with gcc.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]networking\f[R] [boolean]
.RS 2
.PP
When built using \f[CR]solbuild(1)\f[R], access to the network is
removed.
A new loopback device will be provided within the container.
This ensures that packages do not accidently download unverifiable
content during build.
.PP
If for any reason, networking is still required, you can set this key to
\f[CR]yes\f[R].
However, always evaluate whether it is avoidable first.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]environment\f[R] [unicode]
.RS 2
.PP
By default, this key is empty and no additional content is exported to
each build step.
The \f[CR]environment\f[R] key provides an easy method to export content
such as variables to the environment for the entirety of the build
(where the environment is reset between each build step).
.RE
.PP
\f[CR]Build Steps\f[R]
.PP
The build steps are text\-only data values.
\f[CR]ypkg\-build(1)\f[R] will interpret special \(lqmacro\(rq values in
these steps, and evaluate them in a new environment via the
\f[CR]bash(1)\f[R] shell.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]setup\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
Performed immediately after source preparation and extraction.
This is where you should look to patch your package if necessary, and
perform any configuration routines (i.e.\ \f[CR]%configure\f[R])
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]build\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
The main build step.
This is where you compile code and do long running code.
An example would be running \f[CR]%make\f[R]
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]install\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
The install step will install of the built files into the final
installation directory, to be converted into a native \f[CR].eopkg\f[R]
file.
This is where your \f[CR]%make_install\f[R] would happen, for example.
.PP
Remember, this is to install inside the \f[CR]package\f[R].
This doesn\(cqt impact the package installation on another users
computer.
There is no \(lqpostinstall\(rq concept currently supported by ypkg.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]check\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
Run any test suites in this step.
This is the final step in the chain, and allows you to verify what you
just built.
This is a good place to run \f[CR]%make check\f[R]
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]profile\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
If this step is present, then each build set that is enabled (native,
\f[CR]emul32\f[R]), will gain a series of new steps.
The build will be configured for profile guided optimisation, and this
step will be used to execute the PGO workload.
.PP
In essence the workflow looks like this:
.IP
.EX
  \- \(gasetup\(ga as PGO
  \- \(gabuild\(ga as PGO
  \- \(gaprofile\(ga as PGO
  \- Clean happens here.
  \- \(gasetup\(ga to use PGO data
  \- \(gabuild\(ga to use PGO data
  \- \(gainstall\(ga
  \- \(gacheck\(ga
.EE
.PP
The compiler flags will be modifed automatically during each step to
make PGO integration seamless.
For an real world case on how this helps, check out:
.IP \(bu 2
https://clearlinux.org/blogs/profile\-guided\-optimization\-mariadb\-benchmarks
.RE
.PP
\f[CR]Macros\f[R]
.PP
ypkg supports a wide range of macros for easier package building.
They evolve often and quickly, so you should always refer to the main
\f[CR]package.yml\f[R] documentation:
.IP \(bu 2
https://getsol.us/articles/packaging/package.yml/en/#actionable\-macros
.IP \(bu 2
https://getsol.us/articles/packaging/package.yml/en/#variable\-macros
.PP
It may also be beneficial to study the \f[CR]rc.yml\f[R] file defining
the build macros:
.IP \(bu 2
https://github.com/getsolus/ypkg/blob/master/ypkg2/rc.yml
.SS EXAMPLES
\f[CR]A Complete package\f[R]
.IP
.EX
name       : nano
version    : 2.7.1
release    : 63
source     :
    \- https://www.nano\-editor.org/dist/v2.7/nano\-2.7.1.tar.xz : df5cbe69831d7394c0a32fb27373ab313335ea4dc586d6f4be4081eb1de857cd
license    :
    \- GPL\-3.0
summary    : Small, friendly text editor inspired by Pico
component  : editor
description: |
    GNU nano is an easy\-to\-use text editor originally designed as a
    replacement for Pico, the ncurses\-based editor from the non\-free
    mailer package Pine (itself now available under the Apache License
    as Alpine).
setup      : |
    %configure \-\-enable\-utf8 \-\-docdir=/usr/share/doc/nano
build      : |
    %make
install    : |
    %make_install
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]Git sources\f[R]
.IP
.EX
source     :
    \- git|https://github.com/getsolus/ypkg.git : v17.0
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]Multimap examples\f[R]
.IP
.EX
# Set the component for the main package to \(gasystem.base\(ga
component: system.base

# Also setting the main component to \(gasystem.base\(ga
component:
    \- system.base

# Set the component of the main package to system.base, but set the
# component for subpackage \(gadevel\(ga to \(gaprogramming.devel\(ga
component:
    \- system.base
    \- devel: programming.devel

# Rundeps multimap
rundeps:
    \- somepkg
    \- devel: somepkg2

# Rundeps, list as explicit key\(aqs value
rundeps:
    \- somepkg
    \- devel:
        \- somepkg
        \- somepkg2
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]Build dependencies examples\f[R]
.IP
.EX
# Build dependencies, in various flavours:
builddeps:
    \- glibc\-32bit\-devel
    \- pkgconfig(gtk+\-3.0)
    \- pkgconfig32(zlib)
.EE
